No need to handicap them so badly.  Even the 'joke' classes in vanilla
Nethack don't have too many tough restrictions.

First off, Nerds are generally lawful, except when it comes to
copyright violations.  One can overlook this fact.  Obviously, a Nerd
would have experience at playing all alignments (check his old
character sheets from his AD&D campaigns that he's meticulously saved!)
so having a restriction against Lawful characters seems wonky.

Orc Nerds would be amazing (ok, you can restrict lawful Orc Nerds..
that's fine.)

A minor version of the fumbling intrinsic would be ok.  They don't
fumble and drop things ALL the time.  Maybe a super-low chance compared
to normal, with an elevated chance of fumbling when handling breakable
objects or around sleeping monsters.  Either that, or make the fumbling
go away after a certain level (like level 3, 5, or something).

Stat caps on starting characters would be fine, but there's no reason
to believe that a Nerdslayer wouldn't eventually get stronger via the
usual routes.  And sometimes Nerds get very charismatic - see any
number of rich computer business-owning nerds in the media.  The extra
price a Nerd pays at the shop seems a little redundant, especially
since his early Charisma will be rock bottom anyway.  That'd be real
tough on the early game.

Why can't they gain poison resistance?  You'd think they'd START with
intrinsic poison resistance, 2 liters of Jolt a day can't be good for
you.

Myopia is amusing, but why not just make them 'blind' unless wearing
lenses (noncurable)?  Assume their vision is super bad.

I also think that Nerds should start with a +1 lightsaber.  Damage as
regular long sword - light source equivalent to lamp (or one square
radius smaller).  Must be 'invoked' or 'applied', whichever one, after
wielding, otherwise it does 1d2 damage to an attacking monster instead
of normal damage.  Immune to intrinsic fumbling while wielding a
lightsaber, but magical fumbling gives 5% risk of striking self with
sword for standard combat damage.  Chance of deflecting incoming
projectiles based on skill and level, or reflecting rays.

Cloak of Invisibility is.. meh.  Why not intrinsic stealth - nobody
notices them until they do something embarrassing?

Magic marker and t-shirt are good ascension kit items to start with
that help prop up the class a little bit.  Who wouldn't want to ascend
a Nerd?

Quest artifact is retardedly powerful, though, I think it needs a
tone-down - the damage reduction is probably not necessary, and the
luckstone bit would be redundant.  Although obviously any Nethacker
would love to have the Source Code (great idea, by the way), it can't
be THAT powerful.

Also, Nerds are nerds but that doesn't mean they're kids - having them
run around the dungeon with a bunch of toys kind of seems like a drag.
Even Tourists are packing +2 darts and potions of extra healing.  They
need a few strong early items to get them out of the main game and into
the long haul.  A blessed figurine of a particular animal is a decent
idea, although I'm not entirely sure which one to recommend (maybe just
a dog, instead of getting a dog right out the starting gate?)

Also, nerds should use Japanese names for most everything.  You know
how they are with their anime.  <3

Starting with a little protection is ok - you can attribute it to The
Force.

End goal - mix of fighter capabilities with spellcasting.  Average HP
gain per level, equivalent to Rogues or Rangers.  Based on
RPG/Jedi/cosplay nerds.

